{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9559314cd6eb0ea","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tMult\n2\t\n3\tSingle-Turn Spoken Function Calling\n4\tEnv: {\"location\": \"Paris\", \"datetime\": \"2025-04-04 02:35:28, Friday\"}\n5\t\n6\tEnv: {\"location\":\n7\t\n8\tAvailable Spoken Functions: update_reminder_time,\n9\tﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_time, ﬁnd_location_reminder_by_location,\n10\tupdate_recurring_reminder_content, update_location_reminder_time\n11\t\n12\tSpoken Function Calling: A New Perspective on Spoken\n13\tLanguage Understanding for Large Audio Language Models\n14\t\n15\tAvailable Spok\n16\tget_distance_betw\n17\tget_navigation_dir\n18\tﬁnd_recurring_rem\n19\t\n20\tUserʼs Speech Query: I need to update my weekly reminder from “Take\n21\tout the trash” to “Replace the kitchen sponge”, starting this Friday at 7 AM.\n22\tSFC Result: update_recurring_reminder_content(content=\"Take out the\n23\ttrash\", new_content=”Replace the kitchen sponge\", time=\"2025-04-04\n24\t07:00:00\", frequency=\"weekly\")\n25\t\n26\tYuezhang Peng∗\n27\t\n28\tYuxin Liu∗\n29\t\n30\tShanghai Jiao Tong University,\n31\tToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\n32\tHangzhou, China\n33\t\n34\tShanghai Jiao Tong University\n35\tShanghai, China\n36\t\n37\tZhifu Gao\n38\tToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\n39\tHangzhou, China\n40\t\n41\tChangfeng Gao\n42\t\n43\tEnvironment: {\"location\":\n44\t”Paris\", \"datetime\": \"202407-19 19:04:19, Friday\"}\n45\t\n46\tarXiv:2608.05126v1 [cs.CL] 5 Aug 2026\n47\t\n48\tSpoken Language Understanding (SLU) is the core component of\n49\ttask-oriented dialogue systems and a pivotal link in achieving seamless human-agent interaction. While traditional SLU can effectively\n50\textract user semantics for closed-set tasks after in-domain supervised fine-tuning, it faces significant challenges in leveraging incontext learning for open-domain tasks due to its ambiguous rule\n51\tdefinitions. This work proposes Spoken Function Calling (SFC), a\n52\tnovel semantic understanding perspective that optimizes semantic\n53\tunderstanding with structured rule definitions, to evolve beyond\n54\ttraditional closed-set SLU. Specifically, we curate and extend a suite\n55\tof spoken functions based on traditional SLU datasets, construct a\n56\tmulti-agent system to synthesize the SFC-Bench dataset, evaluate\n57\tthe performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Audio Language Models (LALMs), and enhance the SFC capabilities of\n58\tLALMs through post-training. Experiments demonstrate that SFC\n59\toutperforms traditional SLU, substantially enhancing the semantic\n60\textraction accuracy for LLMs and LALMs. 1\n61\t\n62\tCCS Concepts\n63\t• Computing methodologies → Natural language processing;\n64\tSpeech recognition; Discourse, dialogue and pragmatics.\n65\t\n66\tKeywords\n67\tSpoken Language Understanding, Spoken Function Calling, Large\n68\tAudio Language Models, In-Context Learning\n69\t\n70\t1\n71\t\n72\tIntroduction\n73\t\n74\tSpoken Language Understanding (SLU) parses the semantic information of user queries through intent classification and slot\n75\tfilling to facilitate the execution of downstream tasks [35]. This\n76\tparadigm has been extensively deployed in voice assistants across\n77\tsmartphones, smart homes, and automotive systems [3, 21, 25].\n78\tHowever, traditional SLU tasks operate under the assumption that\n79\tmodels possess prior knowledge of all predefined intent and slot\n80\tschemas. While models can memorize these rules via in-domain\n81\tSupervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) to achieve superior performance,\n82\t∗ Equal Contribution.\n83\t† Corresponding author.\n84\t1 The code and dataset are available at https://github.com/QwenAudio/FunResearch/\n85\t\n86\ttree/main/SpokenFC.\n87\t\n88\t{Intent: add_schedule, Slot:\n89\tcontent=dentist appointment,\n90\ttime or start_time ? = … ,\n91\tlocation=Paris}\n92\t\n93\tXie Chen†\n94\t\n95\tI maybe add a schedule\n96\tToken Foundry, AlibabaCould\n97\tfor Group\n98\ta denAst appointment on\n99\tJuly 20th at 3 PM in Paris?\n100\tHangzhou, China\n101\t\n102\tAbstract\n103\t\n104\tAvailable Intents: add_schedule,\n105\tadd_recurring_schedule, …\n106\tAvailable Slots: content, Ame,\n107\tlocaAon, start_Ame, frequency, …\n108\t\n109\tXiangang Li User’s Speech Query:\n110\t\n111\tUserʼs Speech\n112\t123 Queen’s Road\n113\t\n114\tToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\n115\tBeijing, China\n116\tSLU Context\n117\tSLU Confusion\n118\t\n119\tSFC Context\n120\t\n121\tLALM\n122\t\n123\t❓\n124\t\n125\tSFC Success\n126\t\n127\tShanghai Jiao Tong University,\n128\tadd_schedule (\n129\t✅\n130\tShanghai Innovation\n131\tInstitute\n132\tcontent=denAst\n133\tappointment,\n134\tAme=2024-07-20 15:00:00,\n135\tShanghai, China\n136\tlocaAon=Paris)\n137\t\n138\tAvailable Functions:\n139\tadd_schedule (content, time,\n140\tlocation),\n141\tadd_recurring_conference\n142\t(content, start_time, frequency),…\n143\t\n144\tSpeech Query: Could I maybe add a schedule for a dentist appointment on July 20th at 3 PM in Paris?\n145\tEnvironmental Context: Date=2024-07-19 19:04:19, Loc=Paris\n146\t\n147\tTraditional SLU Task\n148\tAvailable Intent & Slot Pool\n149\tadd_schedule\n150\tother intents…\n151\t\n152\tlocation\n153\t\n154\ttime\n155\t\n156\tadd_recur_schedule\n157\t\n158\t\"July 20th\n159\tat 3 PM\"\n160\t\n161\t？\n162\t\n163\t？\n164\t\n165\tother slots…\n166\t\n167\tProposed SFC Task\n168\tAvailable Function Pool\n169\t• add_schedule(content, time, location)\n170\t• add_recur_schedule(content,start_time,\n171\tfrequency) ...\n172\t\n173\tSelected Function:\n174\tcontent\n175\t\n176\tstart_time\n177\t\n178\tSLU CONFUSION\n179\t\n180\tAmbiguous rule definition\n181\t\n182\tSlot values face ambiguity\n183\twhen mapping to overlapping\n184\tcandidates (e.g., \"time\" vs.\n185\t\"start_time\")\n186\t\n187\t• add_schedule (content, time, location)\n188\tdentist\n189\tappoint.\n190\t\n191\t2024-0720 15:00:00\n192\t\n193\tParis\n194\t\n195\tSFC SUCCESS\n196\t\n197\tPrecise structured binding\n198\t\n199\tParameter values are\n200\tdetermined by the specific\n201\tschema constraints within the\n202\tselected function\n203\t\n204\tFigure 1: Comparison of traditional SLU and proposed SFC.\n205\tSFC resolves parameter ambiguity through a precise and\n206\tstructured function definition.\n207\t\n208\tthey struggle with open vocabulary and out-of-domain tasks [25].\n209\tEven state-of-the-art (SOTA) Large Language Models (LLMs) and\n210\tLarge Audio-Language Models (LALMs) fail to achieve satisfactory\n211\tresults on such tasks through In-Context Learning (ICL) [4]. In\n212\tthis context, we define “open-domain\" as the capability of a model\n213\tto understand and execute tasks through ICL based on dynamic\n214\tfunctional descriptions, rather than relying on in-domain SFT to\n215\tpre-memorize SLU schemas.\n216\tRecently, function calling has emerged as a transformative paradigm for semantic parsing and task execution [23, 24]. Unlike\n217\ttraditional SLU, which requires agents to pre-memorize rigid rules,\n218\tfunction calling enables LLMs to interpret function definitions\n219\tdynamically through ICL and structured prompts. This approach facilitates the autonomous invocation of the Model Context Protocol\n220\t(MCP) [11] for diverse open-domain tasks, bypassing the need for\n221\tSFT on specific functions. Inspired by the success of function calling\n222\tin open-domain scenarios, we investigate a pivotal question: Can\n223\tSpoken Function Calling (SFC) evolve beyond traditional SLU\n224\tto enhance the semantic extraction performance of agents?\n225\t\n226\t\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n227\t\n228\tSingle-Turn Spoken Function Calling\n229\t\n230\tPeng et al.\n231\t\n232\tMulti-Turn Spoken Function Calling\n233\t\n234\tEnv: {\"location\": \"Paris\", \"datetime\": \"2025-04-04 02:35:28, Friday\"}\n235\tAvailable Spoken Functions: update_reminder_time,\n236\tﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_time, ﬁnd_location_reminder_by_location,\n237\tupdate_recurring_reminder_content, update_location_reminder_time\n238\tUserʼs Speech Query: I need to update my weekly reminder from “Take\n239\tout the trash” to “Replace the kitchen sponge”, starting this Friday at 7 AM.\n240\tSFC Result: update_recurring_reminder_content(content=\"Take out the\n241\ttrash\", new_content=”Replace the kitchen sponge\", time=\"2025-04-04\n242\t07:00:00\", frequency=\"weekly\")\n243\t\n244\tSingle-Turn Multiple Spoken Function Calling\n245\tEnv: {\"location\": \"Seoul\", \"datetime\": \"2023-02-13 19:04:19, Monday\"}\n246\t\n247\tEnv: {\"location\": \"Hong Kong\", \"datetime\": \"2026-05-31 03:00:33, Sunday\"}\n248\tAvailable Spoken Functions:\n249\tget_distance_between_locations, update_recurring_reminder_time,\n250\tget_navigation_directions, delete_recurring_reminder,\n251\tfind_recurring_reminder_by_keyword, get_travel_time_between_locations\n252\t\n253\tStage 1\n254\tUserʼs Speech Query: Find my reminder, tell me the travel time from\n255\t123 Queen’s Road Central, Hong Kong to Hong Kong International Airport.\n256\tSFC Result:\n257\tget_travel_time_between_locations(location1=“123 Queen’s Road Central,\n258\tHong Kong”, location2=“Hong Kong International Airport”),\n259\tﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_keyword(keyword=\"NAN\")\n260\t\n261\tAvailable Spoken Functions:\n262\tskip_reminder, search_wikipedia, ﬁnd_reminder_by_keyword,\n263\tupdate_location_reminder_time, update_recurring_reminder_content\n264\tUserʼs Speech Query: Could you search Wikipedia for the history of the\n265\tEiffel Tower and then change my weekly reminder from “Check the news” to\n266\t“Read the latest article about it,” starting tomorrow morning at 8 AM?\n267\tSFC Result: search_wikipedia(query=\"history of the Eiﬀel Tower\"),\n268\tupdate_recurring_reminder_content(content=\"Check the news\",\n269\tnew_content=\"Read the latest article about the Eiﬀel Tower\", time=\"2023-0214 08:00:00\", frequency=\"weekly\")\n270\t\n271\tStage 2\n272\tUserʼs Speech Query: Reminder about gym.\n273\tSFC Result:\n274\tget_travel_time_between_locations(location1=“123 Queen’s Road Central,\n275\tHong Kong”, location2=“Hong Kong International Airport”),\n276\tﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_keyword(keyword=\"gym\")\n277\t\n278\tFigure 2: Example of Multi-Intent and Multi-Turn Spoken Function Calling tasks.\n279\tHowever, addressing this question presents a critical challenge:\n280\tthe lack of SFC datasets that correspond to traditional SLU\n281\ttasks. Specifically, existing LLMs function call datasets focus on\n282\tcomplex reasoning within code or mathematics, which does not\n283\talign with the requirements of traditional SLU. Furthermore, queries\n284\tand tools designed for text-centric tasks lack the inherent uncertainty and linguistic flexibility of human speech.\n285\tIn this work, we leverage SLU datasets and a multi-agent system\n286\tto construct SFC-Bench, the first large-scale SFC dataset. Through\n287\tcomparative experiments, we demonstrate that SFC is a more suitable perspective for LALM agents than traditional SLU, as shown\n288\tin Figure 1. We further establish a benchmark to investigate the\n289\tperformance and limitations of LLMs and LALMs on SFC tasks of\n290\tvarying levels. In summary, our key contributions are listed below.\n291\t• Perspective Shift: We propose Spoken Function Calling as a\n292\tnovel perspective for task-oriented spoken semantics extraction,\n293\tto extend traditional closed-set SLU based on intent classification\n294\tand slot filling in the agent era.\n295\t• Post-training Optimization: We investigate reinforcement\n296\tlearning (RL) based post-training strategies for LALMs and propose a fine-grained reward method. Leveraging these strategies,\n297\twe develop SpokenFC-7B through post-training on a comprehensive synthetic dataset.\n298\t• Dataset Construction: We introduce SFC-Bench, the first largescale SFC dataset. Starting from a curated tool set of 300 spoken\n299\tfunctions derived from existing SLU datasets, we employ a multiagent system to synthesize multi-level queries and labels.\n300\t• Empirical Validation: Our experiments confirm that: 1) SFC\n301\tserves as a more effective semantic understanding method for\n302\t\n303\tLALMs compared to traditional SLU. 2) Even SOTA LALMs still\n304\tface challenges in high-level SFC tasks involving multi-intent and\n305\tambiguous semantics. 3) SpokenFC-7B outperforms powerful\n306\tclosed-source models on SFC tasks while maintaining robust\n307\tperformance on normal tasks such as speech recognition and\n308\taudio reasoning.\n309\t\n310\t2 Related Work\n311\t2.1 Traditional SLU\n312\t2.1.1 LLMs for SLU. LLMs pre-trained under the scaling law [14]\n313\texhibit emergent reasoning and generalization capabilities across\n314\tvarious Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, successfully addressing complex problems in question-answering [47], mathematics [20], and coding [6]. Recent research explores the application\n315\tof LLMs to SLU tasks. Early research [9] investigates using ICL\n316\tfor intent classification and slot filling, demonstrating that while\n317\tLLMs perform commendably in intent classification, they still face\n318\tchallenges with the more complex structural definitions of slot filling. Whisma [17] employs a Whisper [31] encoder and Llama-3 [8]\n319\tdecoder with modality alignment training, enabling zero-shot SLU\n320\tcapabilities that outperform traditional pipeline methods. Furthermore, MAC-SLU [25] establishes a unified benchmark for LLMs and\n321\tLALMs on SLU, indicating that while current LLMs and LALMs can\n322\tperform well on sub-tasks via ICL (e.g., isolated intent classification\n323\tor slot filling), their overall accuracy remains low and significantly\n324\tlags behind models optimized through SFT.\n325\t2.1.2 SLU Datasets. SLU datasets primarily include single-intent\n326\tbenchmarks such as ATIS, SNIPS, FSC, and SLURP [3, 7, 10, 21],\n327\t\n328\t\fSpoken Function Calling\n329\t\n330\tas well as multi-intent datasets like MixATIS and MixSNIPS [29].\n331\tThe ATIS [10] dataset focuses on simple voice queries for flight\n332\tinformation with only 16 intent categories. SNIPS [7] and FSC [21]\n333\ttarget smart home scenarios but remain limited in scope, featuring\n334\tonly 7 and 13 intents, respectively. The simplicity and homogeneity\n335\tof ATIS, SNIPS, and FSC allow even non-LLM-based SLU models\n336\tto achieve over 95% overall accuracy [28]. SLURP [3] is currently\n337\tthe most widely used large-scale SLU dataset, expanding the intent\n338\tcategories to 46. In the context of Chinese SLU, MAC-SLU [25] is\n339\tderived from text commands in the automotive cabin with speech\n340\tsynthesized via Text-to-Speech (TTS) models. MAC-SLU comprises\n341\t8 domains, 81 intents, and 192 slots, including multi-intent data\n342\twith up to five concurrent intents, surpassing previous datasets\n343\tin both diversity and complexity. Furthermore, although dialogue\n344\tstate tracking [38, 39] is inherently linked with SLU, and existing benchmarks like MTOP [16] and MultiWOZ [5] have adopted\n345\tfunction-calling structures, their scope is restricted to text-based,\n346\tmulti-turn dialogues. In contrast, this work concentrates on endto-end semantic understanding specifically for the speech modality\n347\tand the precise invocation of spoken tools, presenting fundamental\n348\tdifferences in both input modality and task objectives.\n349\t\n350\t2.2\n351\t\n352\tFunction Calling\n353\t\n354\t2.2.1 LLMs for Function Calling. Function calling empowers LLMs\n355\twith tool-use capabilities to overcome inherent limitations such\n356\tas hallucinations [13] and the lack of real-time information. ReAct [43] represents a milestone in function calling, proposing an\n357\tinterleaved reasoning and acting paradigm. This approach guides\n358\tmodels to generate reasoning traces before executing an action,\n359\tleveraging Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [37] to plan tool calls and enhancing the model’s ability to solve complex problems. Toolformer\n360\t[32] introduces a self-supervised learning method that automatically inserts API calls into text and filters low-quality samples,\n361\tteaching the model how to invoke external APIs without extensive\n362\thuman annotation. ToolRL [27] utilizes RL training to bolster the\n363\treasoning capabilities of LLMs during function calling, achieving\n364\tperformance superior to both base and SFT models.\n365\t2.2.2 Function Calling Datasets. Current function calling datasets\n366\tinclude the Berkeley Function Calling Leaderboard (BFCL) [24],\n367\tAPI-Bank [18], ToolBench [30], and ToolACE [19]. BFCL [24] evaluates function calling using a comprehensive dataset encompassing\n368\tHuggingFace, TorchHub, and TensorHub APIs. API-Bank [18] provides the first benchmark specifically for tool-augmented LLMs,\n369\tcovering capabilities ranging from single-turn queries to multi-turn\n370\tdialogue management. ToolLLM [30] constructs ToolBench, a largescale instruction-tuning dataset featuring over 16,000 functional\n371\tRESTful APIs. ToolACE [19] utilizes an automated agentic pipeline\n372\tto generate accurate, complex, and diverse tool-learning data, enhancing function calling through data augmentation. Despite the\n373\tproliferation of these datasets, they remain focused on code, mathematics, and complex reasoning, which do not align with spoken\n374\tsemantic understanding scenarios. Furthermore, speech-centric\n375\tdatasets like StepEval-Audio-Toolcall [40] support only four categories of tools, which is insufficient for a comprehensive evaluation\n376\tof LALMs’ performance in SFC tasks.\n377\t\n378\tMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n379\t\n380\t3 Methodology\n381\t3.1 Spoken Function Calling\n382\tWithin the paradigm of ICL for LLMs and LALMs, the SLU based\n383\ton intent classification and slot filling can be conceptualized as a\n384\tsemantic extraction and generation process grounded in instruction\n385\tfollowing. Its task mapping relationship can be defined as:\n386\t𝐹 SLU : (𝐴, 𝑆 int, 𝑆 slot ) → 𝑌SLU,\n387\t\n388\t(1)\n389\t\n390\twhere 𝐴 denotes the input raw speech signal, 𝑆 int and 𝑆 slot denote\n391\tthe predefined sets of candidate intent and slot labels, respectively.\n392\tThe output 𝑌SLU is represented as a set of semantic frames:\n393\t𝑌SLU = {𝐼 1, . . . , 𝐼𝑛 , (𝑠 1, 𝑣 1 ), . . . , (𝑠𝑚 , 𝑣𝑚 )}.\n394\t\n395\t(2)\n396\t\n397\tIn this structure, 𝐼𝑖 ∈ 𝑆 int is the 𝑖-th identified intent label, while\n398\t𝑠 𝑗 ∈ 𝑆 slot and 𝑣 𝑗 represent the corresponding slot name and its\n399\tassociated value, respectively.\n400\tTo accommodate the requirements for agents to handle opendomain SLU with customized intents and slots and to circumvent\n401\tthe reliance on memorizing rigid rules through SFT, this work\n402\tproposes a novel perspective: Spoken Function Calling. SFC aims\n403\tto map spoken instructions into a sequence of directly executable\n404\tstructured API calls:\n405\t𝐹 SFC : (𝐴, 𝐸, 𝐷 func ) → 𝑌SFC .\n406\t\n407\t(3)\n408\t\n409\tIn this definition, 𝐸 explicitly introduces the environmental context,\n410\twhich serves to compensate for semantic omissions common in\n411\tspoken language; 𝐷 func represents the function definition schema\n412\tfollowing the OpenFunction protocol. The detailed output 𝑌SFC is\n413\tmanifested as a set of structured function calls:\n414\t𝑌SFC = {𝑓𝑖 (𝑘𝑖,1 = 𝑣𝑖,1, 𝑘𝑖,2 = 𝑣𝑖,2, . . . , 𝑘𝑖,𝑚𝑖 = 𝑣𝑖,𝑚𝑖 )}𝑛𝑖=1,\n415\t\n416\t(4)\n417\t\n418\twhere 𝑓𝑖 is the retrieved function name, and 𝑘𝑖,𝑗 and 𝑣𝑖,𝑗 represent\n419\tparameter keys and values. If a parameter is missing, it is assigned\n420\ta value of NAN to trigger subsequent completion mechanisms. Compared to traditional SLU, the SFC perspective offers the following\n421\tsignificant advantages: 1) Robustness of Structured Constraints:\n422\tThe schema constraints introduced via 𝐷 func serve as strong prior\n423\tknowledge, effectively resolving the boundary ambiguity issues encountered by traditional SLU when performing complex parameter\n424\textraction. 2) Coupled Intent and Slot Parsing: The function\n425\tdefinition 𝐷 func simultaneously constrains the parsing of both function names (intents) and parameter values (slots). This addresses a\n426\tcritical limitation in traditional SLU, where the correlation between\n427\tintents and slots is difficult to define explicitly.\n428\t\n429\t3.2\n430\t\n431\tRL-based Post-training\n432\t\n433\tThis study adopts Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) [34]\n434\tas the core algorithm for post-training. Compared to the traditional\n435\tProximal Policy Optimization (PPO) [33] algorithm, a significant\n436\tadvantage of GRPO is that it eliminates the need to train an additional large-scale value function model and substantially reduces\n437\tthe memory footprint and computational overhead.\n438\tFor each spoken query 𝑞, GRPO samples a group of outputs\n439\t{𝑜 1, 𝑜 2, . . . , 𝑜𝐺 } from the old policy 𝜋𝜃 old . The original objective\n440\t\n441\t\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n442\t\n443\tPeng et al.\n444\t\n445\tSFC Data Synthesis\n446\t\n447\tMulti-Agent Generator\n448\t\n449\tHuman-Agent Validation\n450\t\n451\tEnvironmental\n452\tContext\n453\t\n454\tSLU\n455\tDataset\n456\t\n457\tFunction\n458\tMetadata\n459\t\n460\tMultiple\n461\tAgents\n462\t\n463\tQuery Agent\n464\t\n465\tLabel Agent\n466\t\n467\tGenerate\n468\t\n469\tSpeech Agent\n470\t\n471\tValidator Agent\n472\t\n473\tQuery Filtering\n474\tLabel Check\n475\tStyle Adaptation\n476\t……\n477\t\n478\tValidate\n479\t\n480\tComplexity Levels\n481\t\n482\tHuman Evaluation\n483\t\n484\tSpoken Function Call\n485\tDataset\n486\tSingle-Intent SFC\n487\t\n488\tMulti-Intent SFC\n489\t\n490\tMulti-Turn SFC\n491\t\n492\tTrain Set\n493\t\n494\tTest Set\n495\t\n496\tFigure 3: Multi-Agent System for Data Construction.\n497\tfunction 𝐽GRPO 