del struggles to hit all structured elements simultaneously,\n719\tSecondly, we leveraged Gemini-2.5 Pro to transform these scethereby causing slow convergence.\n720\tnarios into standardized function definitions. To ensure functional\n721\tTo mitigate this, we propose a Fine-Grained reward method\n722\tdiversity and logical complexity, we referenced established textby decomposing the SFC task into three core sub-tasks: function\n723\tbased function-calling benchmarks such as BFCL [24] and ToolACE\n724\tname recognition, parameter key matching, and parameter value\n725\t[19]. The generated functions cover a broad spectrum of operations,\n726\textraction. By providing positive feedback when a sub-task is perranging from standard CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) actions\n727\tformed correctly, we incentivize the model to master individual\n728\tto domain-specific complex logic. Thirdly, we conducted manual\n729\tcomponents before achieving full function call synthesis. The final\n730\tRefinement, where every generated function underwent rigorous\n731\treward 𝑅Fine-Grained is normalized to the [−1, 1] range by scaling the\n732\thuman filtering and correction.\n733\t\n734\t\fSpoken Function Calling\n735\t\n736\tMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n737\t\n738\tl\n739\t\n740\tGenefora\n741\tIn\n742\t(22)\n743\t\n744\tRemin\n745\t(24) der\n746\t\n747\tCo\n748\tnf\n749\t(20eren\n750\t) ce\n751\t\n752\tM\n753\t(11ovie\n754\t)\n755\t\n756\tket\n757\tTic(12)\n758\t\n759\tShoOnline\n760\tp\n761\t(18)ping\n762\t\n763\tPersonal\n764\tAssistant\n765\t(90)\n766\t\n767\tAlarm\n768\t(14)\n769\t\n770\tLife\n771\tServices\n772\t(45)\n773\t\n774\tFood\n775\tDelivery\n776\t(14)\n777\t\n778\tr\n779\tnda\n780\tCale(20)\n781\t\n782\tInformation\n783\tRetrieval\n784\t(45)\n785\t\n786\tTodo\n787\t(12)\n788\t\n789\tan\n790\tRestaur\n791\t(13)\n792\t\n793\tCommunication\n794\t(56)\n795\t\n796\tMe\n797\ts\n798\t(9) sage\n799\t\n800\ti\n801\tTax11)\n802\t(\n803\tT\n804\t(13rain\n805\t)\n806\t\n807\tPho\n808\t(12)ne\n809\t\n810\thip\n811\tt S(10)\n812\t\n813\tTravel &\n814\tTransport\n815\t(64)\n816\t\n817\tPla\n818\t(14)ne\n819\t\n820\tHotel\n821\t(16)\n822\t\n823\tApp\n824\tCom4m) .\n825\t(1\n826\t\n827\teo\n828\tVidCall )\n829\t(10\n830\tl\n831\ti\n832\tEm1a1)\n833\t(\n834\t\n835\tMetrics\n836\t\n837\tTrain\n838\t\n839\tTest-ID\n840\t\n841\tTest-OOD\n842\t\n843\tLevel 1\n844\tAvg. Question Length\n845\tAvg. Answer Length\n846\tAvg. Audio Length\n847\t\n848\t774.58\n849\t20.68\n850\t7.30\n851\t\n852\t728.41\n853\t18.35\n854\t6.62\n855\t\n856\t704.69\n857\t13.34\n858\t6.69\n859\t\n860\tLevel 2\n861\tAvg. Question Length\n862\tAvg. Answer Length\n863\tAvg. Audio Length\n864\t\n865\t708.80\n866\t30.14\n867\t10.65\n868\t\n869\t707.85\n870\t28.51\n871\t10.28\n872\t\n873\t744.68\n874\t26.93\n875\t10.21\n876\t\n877\tLevel 3-1\n878\tAvg. Question Length\n879\tAvg. Answer Length\n880\tAvg. Audio Length\n881\t\n882\t792.51\n883\t40.92\n884\t8.70\n885\t\n886\t808.55\n887\t44.93\n888\t8.65\n889\t\n890\t732.21\n891\t27.68\n892\t7.66\n893\t\n894\tLevel 3-2\n895\tAvg. Question Length\n896\tAvg. Answer Length\n897\tAvg. Audio Length\n898\t\n899\t827.57\n900\t73.96\n901\t13.15\n902\t\n903\t806.98\n904\t63.17\n905\t11.55\n906\t\n907\t793.87\n908\t52.71\n909\t11.32\n910\t\n911\tFigure 4: The domain distribution (left) and detailed statistics (right) of average question lengths (tokens), answer lengths\n912\t(tokens), and audio lengths (seconds) across different levels of splits.\n913\tTable 1: Sample distribution in the SFC-Bench dataset.\n914\t\n915\tLevel Train\n916\t\n917\tTest-ID\n918\t\n919\tTest-OOD\n920\t\n921\tMulti- MultiIntent Turn\n922\t\n923\tL1\n924\tL2\n925\tL3-1\n926\tL3-2\n927\t\n928\t2,647\n929\t817\n930\t447\n931\t588\n932\t\n933\t902\n934\t480\n935\t317\n936\t287\n937\t\n938\t321\n939\t94\n940\t126\n941\t52\n942\t\n943\t✗\n944\t✓\n945\t✗\n946\t✓\n947\t\n948\t✗\n949\t✗\n950\t✓\n951\t✓\n952\t\n953\tTotal\n954\t\n955\t4,499\n956\t\n957\t1,986\n958\t\n959\t593\n960\t\n961\t–\n962\t\n963\t–\n964\t\n965\tIn total, we curated 300 daily-life oriented spoken tools spanning\n966\t5 domains and 21 scenarios, as presented in Figure 4 (left). To\n967\tevaluate the model’s generalization capabilities, we partitioned the\n968\tfunction set into 232 In-Distribution (ID) and 68 Out-of-Distribution\n969\t(OOD) functions. Although the diversity and inherent complexity\n970\tof the Test-OOD set are slightly lower than those of the Test-ID\n971\tset due to this distribution, it does not compromise the validity of\n972\tthe experiments in assessing the model’s training generalization.\n973\tAll subsequent training was conducted solely on data synthesized\n974\tfrom the ID subset, while evaluations were performed on both ID\n975\tand OOD test sets to verify robustness and transferability.\n976\t\n977\t4.2\n978\t\n979\tSFC-Bench Construction\n980\t\n981\tWorkflow Overview. The workflow for SFC-Bench construction\n982\tis depicted in Figure 3. We synthesize the dataset by leveraging\n983\tfunction metadata curated from SLU benchmarks, integrated with\n984\tdiverse environmental contexts to facilitate data augmentation.\n985\t\n986\tMulti-Agent Generator. Central to this workflow is the MultiAgent Generator, powered by GPT-OSS-120B [1] with custom system prompts, which comprises three primary functional agents: 1)\n987\tQuery Agent: Generates text queries based on pre-selected functions and environmental context. For Level 3 data involving ambiguous semantics, an additional step of fuzzy rewriting is performed.\n988\t2) Label Agent: Produces accurate function calls based on the chosen functions, environmental context, and queries. For ambiguous\n989\tLevel 3 queries, it generates function calls containing NAN parameters to assess potential hallucination. 3) Speech Agent: Introduces\n990\tspoken language features (e.g., redundancy, self-corrections) and\n991\tverbalizes technical formats (e.g., converting “abc@gmail.com\" to\n992\t“abc at gmail dot com\"). Finally, it employs IndexTTS-2 [46] to synthesize speech queries by utilizing speaker timbre profiles from the\n993\tLibriSpeech dataset [22] to mimic diverse vocal characteristics. To\n994\tensure experimental rigor, distinct speaker identities are assigned\n995\tto the training, validation, and test sets, maintaining strict speaker\n996\tisolation across the data splits.\n997\tValidator. Data synthesized by the Multi-Agent Generator undergoes inspection and filtering by the validator agent to ensure\n998\tlogical and grammatical soundness, as well as consistency between\n999\tqueries and labels. For the test set, an additional human validation phase is conducted to guarantee quality. The distribution of\n1000\tthe SFC-Bench is summarized in Table 1. We generated over 7,000\n1001\tSFC data samples, covering a range of Multi-Intent and Multi-Turn\n1002\tscenarios.\n1003\tStatistical data in Figure 4 (right) demonstrate a trend in complexity as difficulty levels escalate. The average answer length\n1004\tsurges from 20 tokens at Level 1 to over 70 tokens at Level 3-2,\n1005\thighlighting the stringent requirements for structured parsing in\n1006\tmulti-intent and multi-turn semantic completion tasks. Concurrently, the mean audio duration increases from about 7 seconds\n1007\t\n1008\t\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1009\t\n1010\tPeng et al.\n1011\t\n1012\tTable 2: Qualitative comparison between SFC and traditional SLU tasks. The Result column indicates the success (✓) or failure\n1013\t(✗) of each task based on the ground truth. Blue text highlights specific discrepancies.\n1014\tTask\n1015\t\n1016\tModel Prediction\n1017\t\n1018\tGround Truth\n1019\t\n1020\tResult\n1021\t\n1022\tCase 1: Hey, delete my doctor appointment reminder for tomorrow morning at 8 AM, I’m in a rush!\n1023\tSFC\n1024\tSLU\n1025\t\n1026\tdelete_reminder(content = \"doctor appointment\", time = \"202503-20 08:00:00\")\n1027\t\"intent\": \"delete_reminder\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"doctor appointment\" }\n1028\t\n1029\tdelete_reminder(content = \"doctor appointment\", time = \"202503-20 08:00:00\")\n1030\t\"intent\": \"delete_reminder\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"doctor appointment\", \"time\": \"2025-03-20 08:00:00\" }\n1031\t\n1032\t✓\n1033\t✗\n1034\t\n1035\tCase 2: Um, could I maybe add a schedule for a dentist appointment on July 20th at 3 PM in Paris?\n1036\tSFC\n1037\tSLU\n1038\t\n1039\tadd_schedule(content = \"dentist appointment\", time = \"2024-0720 15:00:00\", location=\"Paris\")\n1040\t\"intent\": \"add_schedule\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"dentist appointment\",\n1041\t\"start_time\": \"2024-07-20 15:00:00\", \"location\": \"Paris\" }\n1042\t\n1043\tTable 3: Experimental results of SLU and SFC tasks. The metrics include Intent (Function Name) accuracy, Slot (Parameter\n1044\tValue) F1 Score, and Overall Accuracy across ID and OOD test\n1045\tsets. Best results are in bold.\n1046\tTest-ID\n1047\tTask Intent\n1048\t\n1049\tSlot\n1050\t\n1051\tTest-OOD\n1052\tOverall Intent\n1053\t\n1054\tSlot\n1055\t\n1056\tOverall\n1057\t\n1058\t69.73\n1059\t85.70\n1060\t\n1061\t97.20\n1062\t99.07\n1063\t\n1064\t92.52\n1065\t95.83\n1066\t\n1067\t83.80\n1068\t91.90\n1069\t\n1070\t84.26\n1071\t92.68\n1072\t\n1073\t70.44\n1074\t87.03\n1075\t\n1076\t96.88\n1077\t99.07\n1078\t\n1079\t90.24\n1080\t94.48\n1081\t\n1082\t82.24\n1083\t90.03\n1084\t\n1085\tQwen2.5-7B, Text Input\n1086\tSLU\n1087\t93.90 78.73\n1088\tICL\n1089\tSFC\n1090\t91.57 85.41\n1091\t\n1092\t61.86\n1093\t73.95\n1094\t\n1095\t93.15\n1096\t93.46\n1097\t\n1098\t82.71\n1099\t87.58\n1100\t\n1101\t70.57\n1102\t75.70\n1103\t\n1104\t68.63\n1105\t83.15\n1106\t\n1107\t93.77\n1108\t99.38\n1109\t\n1110\t87.42\n1111\t94.48\n1112\t\n1113\t77.00\n1114\t89.72\n1115\t\n1116\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B, Speech Input\n1117\tSLU\n1118\t94.46 68.06\n1119\t53.88\n1120\tICL\n1121\tSFC\n1122\t93.02 79.69 63.86\n1123\t\n1124\t95.64\n1125\t97.20\n1126\t\n1127\t69.12\n1128\t80.43\n1129\t\n1130\t50.16\n1131\t68.54\n1132\t\n1133\t96.57\n1134\t100.0\n1135\t\n1136\t74.79\n1137\t79.52\n1138\t\n1139\t57.01\n1140\t67.29\n1141\t\n1142\tQwen3-8B, Text Input\n1143\tSLU\n1144\t96.01 85.20\n1145\tICL\n1146\tSFC\n1147\t97.56 91.83\n1148\tSFT\n1149\t\n1150\tSFT\n1151\t\n1152\tSFT\n1153\t\n1154\tSLU\n1155\tSFC\n1156\t\n1157\tSLU\n1158\tSFC\n1159\t\n1160\tSLU\n1161\tSFC\n1162\t\n1163\t96.74\n1164\t97.67\n1165\t\n1166\t95.79\n1167\t96.78\n1168\t\n1169\t98.56\n1170\t99.45\n1171\t\n1172\t84.94\n1173\t90.97\n1174\t\n1175\t85.50\n1176\t87.80\n1177\t\n1178\t75.61\n1179\t80.71\n1180\t\n1181\tTable 4: Comparison of average confidence (Log Probabilities)\n1182\tbetween SFC and SLU tasks.\n1183\t\n1184\tSFC Confidence\n1185\tSLU Confidence\n1186\t\n1187\tTest-ID\n1188\t\n1189\tTest-OOD\n1190\t\n1191\t-1.62\n1192\t-1.87\n1193\t\n1194\t-1.57\n1195\t-1.72\n1196\t\n1197\tto 13 seconds. This increased audio load substantially exacerbates\n1198\tthe challenges of ASR error propagation and long-range semantic\n1199\talignment, which constitute the primary obstacles to maintaining\n1200\trobustness in LALMs when processing complex instructions.\n1201\t\n1202\tadd_schedule(content = \"dentist appointment\", time = \"2024-0720 15:00:00\", location=\"Paris\")\n1203\t\"intent\": \"add_schedule\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"dentist appointment\",\n1204\t\"time\": \"2024-07-20 15:00:00\", \"location\": \"Paris\" }\n1205\t\n1206\t5\n1207\t\n1208\t✓\n1209\t✗\n1210\t\n1211\tExperiments\n1212\t\n1213\tExperiments are organized into three components: 1) SLU-SFC\n1214\tComparative Analysis: compare the performance of models when\n1215\tprocessing identical semantic datasets under traditional SLU and\n1216\tour proposed SFC perspective. 2) Benchmark Study: evaluate\n1217\tLLMs and LALMs’ performance on SFC-Bench across varying levels\n1218\tof difficulty. 3) Post-Training: enhance the SFC capabilities of\n1219\tLALMs by integrating SFT and RL techniques, while maintaining\n1220\tmodels’ general capabilities.\n1221\t\n1222\t5.1\n1223\t\n1224\tSLU vs SFC\n1225\t\n1226\tWe initially conducted comparative experiments using the Level 1\n1227\tsubset of SFC-Bench. These experiments involved both ICL and SFT\n1228\tacross LLMs Qwen2.5-7B and Qwen3-8B [42], and LALM Qwen2.5Omni-7B [41]. For LLMs, the inputs consisted of textual user queries,\n1229\twhereas the LALM received the corresponding speech input with\n1230\tthe same context. All models were deployed by vLLM [15] framework to accelerate inference. In the SFT phase, we utilized the LlamaFactory [45] training framework and applied Low-Rank Adaptation\n1231\t(LoRA) [12] for parameter-efficient fine-tuning.\n1232\t5.1.1 Quantitative Analysis. SFC consistently outperforms the\n1233\ttraditional SLU, across both text and audio modalities. As\n1234\tshown in Table 3, SFC demonstrates an improvement of 5–18%\n1235\tin both audio and text modalities. These gains are primarily attributed to the more granular and rigorous function definitions\n1236\tinherent in SFC, which enhance the accuracy of parameter value\n1237\t(analogous to the slot filling in traditional SLU), thereby boosting\n1238\tthe overall performance. In contrast, SFC exhibits no significant\n1239\tadvantage in function name (equivalent to intent classification in\n1240\tSLU). The reason is that both function name and intent classification are relatively straightforward classification problems that do\n1241\tnot necessitate complex rule constraints.\n1242\t5.1.2 Qualitative Analysis. Core advantage of SFC over traditional SLU lies in its more structured rule definitions. SFC\n1243\tutilizes explicit schemas for tools and parameters; for any given tool,\n1244\tthe parameter rules are fixed. For instance, as shown in Table 2, the\n1245\tdelete_reminder tool is strictly defined with two parameters: content\n1246\tand time. In traditional SLU, however, the rules for intent classification and slot filling are often decoupled. For a specific intent, the\n1247\t\n1248\t\fSpoken Function Calling\n1249\t\n1250\tMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1251\t\n1252\tTable 5: Experimental results on the SFC Test-ID. The metrics include Intent (Function Name) Accuracy, Slot (Parameter Value)\n1253\tF1 Score, and Overall Accuracy. Best results are in bold.\n1254\tLevel 1\n1255\t\n1256\tLevel 2\n1257\t\n1258\tLevel 3-1\n1259\t\n1260\tLevel 3-2\n1261\t\n1262\tAverage (Turn-Level)\n1263\t\n1264\tIntent Slot Overall\n1265\t\n1266\tIntent Slot Overall\n1267\t\n1268\tIntent Slot Overall\n1269\t\n1270\tIntent Slot Overall\n1271\t\n1272\tIntent Slot Overall\n1273\t\n1274\tLLM\n1275\t\n1276\tLlama-3.1-8B-Instruct\n1277\tQwen2.5-7B\n1278\tQwen3-8B\n1279\tQwen3-32B\n1280\t\n1281\t68.40 56.78\n1282\t91.57 85.41\n1283\t97.56 92.99\n1284\t94.01 91.24\n1285\t\n1286\t34.48\n1287\t73.95\n1288\t87.69\n1289\t81.71\n1290\t\n1291\t57.17 44.58\n1292\t87.97 87.02\n1293\t94.09 93.63\n1294\t92.41 93.92\n1295\t\n1296\t7.59\n1297\t61.81\n1298\t80.39\n1299\t81.22\n1300\t\n1301\t50.79 52.84\n1302\t85.17 77.02\n1303\t95.27 80.99\n1304\t92.43 81.44\n1305\t\n1306\t9.46\n1307\t27.44\n1308\t31.34\n1309\t37.33\n1310\t\n1311\t52.61 55.52\n1312\t79.09 80.58\n1313\t83.62 80.56\n1314\t87.11 82.30\n1315\t\n1316\t5.23\n1317\t19.86\n1318\t22.95\n1319\t26.04\n1320\t\n1321\t64.71 50.52\n1322\t89.55 78.87\n1323\t94.42 83.77\n1324\t92.97 84.42\n1325\t\n1326\t20.86\n1327\t54.89\n1328\t62.83\n1329\t64.02\n1330\t\n1331\tASR +\n1332\tLLM\n1333\t\n1334\tLlama-3.1-8B-Instruct\n1335\tQwen2.5-Omni\n1336\tQwen3-8B\n1337\tQwen3-32B\n1338\t\n1339\t68.51 45.29\n1340\t93.02 79.69\n1341\t97.67 78.77\n1342\t94.90 78.90\n1343\t\n1344\t27.61\n1345\t63.86\n1346\t68.07\n1347\t64.63\n1348\t\n1349\t56.96 37.47\n1350\t82.49 76.74\n1351\t92.83 76.58\n1352\t92.19 77.94\n1353\t\n1354\t5.91\n1355\t47.05\n1356\t49.58\n1357\t51.27\n1358\t\n1359\t49.84 45.42\n1360\t80.44 73.07\n1361\t94.64 73.95\n1362\t91.17 78.49\n1363\t\n1364\t5.99\n1365\t18.61\n1366\t17.98\n1367\t23.77\n1368\t\n1369\t44.95 49.53\n1370\t40.07 66.76\n1371\t86.41 74.40\n1372\t86.76 78.70\n1373\t\n1374\t1.74\n1375\t7.32\n1376\t13.94\n1377\t16.63\n1378\t\n1379\t63.88 42.97\n1380\t84.02 73.85\n1381\t94.29 72.97\n1382\t92.66 76.01\n1383\t\n1384\t15.90\n1385\t48.80\n1386\t47.27\n1387\t48.96\n1388\t\n1389\tLALM\n1390\t\n1391\tQwen2.5-Omni\n1392\tQwen3-Omni\n1393\tGemini-2.5-Flash\n1394\tGemini-2.5-Pro\n1395\tGPT-4o-Audio\n1396\t\n1397\t93.46 81.67\n1398\t86.47 81.69\n1399\t98.56 85.61\n1400\t95.23 87.12\n1401\t98.89 88.12\n1402\t\n1403\t65.19\n1404\t72.06\n1405\t77.17\n1406\t78.05\n1407\t80.27\n1408\t\n1409\t80.38 76.45\n1410\t86.08 82.70\n1411\t90.72 80.81\n1412\t90.08 83.24\n1413\t92.83 84.84\n1414\t\n1415\t47.68\n1416\t60.55\n1417\t55.91\n1418\t63.29\n1419\t64.56\n1420\t\n1421\t73.50 69.56\n1422\t49.21 71.30\n1423\t81.07 82.04\n1424\t88.64 85.06\n1425\t89.27 84.12\n1426\t\n1427\t17.35\n1428\t8.20\n1429\t35.02\n1430\t47.95\n1431\t35.96\n1432\t\n1433\t46.69 68.35\n1434\t46.34 73.76\n1435\t67.94 81.80\n1436\t73.52 81.62\n1437\t77.00 83.53\n1438\t\n1439\t8.01\n1440\t13.24\n1441\t25.09\n1442\t35.19\n1443\t30.31\n1444\t\n1445\t83.59 73.84\n1446\t78.83 77.23\n1447\t91.45 82.60\n1448\t91.25 84.21\n1449\t94.08 85.40\n1450\t\n1451\t49.03\n1452\t55.07\n1453\t64.40\n1454\t69.00\n1455\t68.30\n1456\t\n1457\tCategory Model\n1458\t\n1459\tTable 6: Error case analysis of GPT-4o-Audio on the Level 3 test set. Blue text highlights hallucinated dates or improperly\n1460\tformatted natural language responses produced by the model.\n1461\tID\n1462\t\n1463\tUser Speech Request\n1464\t\n1465\tModel Prediction\n1466\t\n1467\tGround Truth\n1468\t\n1469\tCase 1\n1470\t\n1471\tHey, could you set a reminder for me to\n1472\tpick up the dry cleaning this Friday?\n1473\t\n1474\t[add_reminder( content = \"pick up the dry cleaning\", time = \"2025-12-12 00:00:00\" )]\n1475\t\n1476\t[add_reminder(content = \"pick up the dry cleaning\", time = \"NAN\" )]\n1477\t\n1478\tCase 2\n1479\t\n1480\tUm, could you maybe change the destination for my train order? I’d like the\n1481\tnew destination to be Geelong.\n1482\t\n1483\tPlease provide the order ID for the train order you’d\n1484\tlike to update with the new destination.\n1485\t\n1486\t[update_train_order_dest (order_id = \"NAN\",\n1487\tdestination = \"Geelong\")]\n1488\t\n1489\tassociated slots are not explicitly constrained, and the extraction\n1490\tresults depend entirely on the semantic information present in the\n1491\tuser’s query. This higher degree of freedom frequently leads to\n1492\televated error rates.\n1493\tStructured SFC definitions provide the model with higher\n1494\tgeneration confidence. We further compared the output confidence of the Qwen2.5-Omni-7B model during inference for both\n1495\tSFC and SLU tasks. As illustrated in the Table 4, the model exhibits\n1496\tsignificantly higher Log Probabilities when performing the SFC task,\n1497\tindicating superior decoding confidence, which ultimately leads to\n1498\ta higher task success rate.\n1499\t\n1500\t5.2\n1501\t\n1502\tBenchmark\n1503\t\n1504\tConsistent with the comparative experiments, all open-source models were deployed using the vLLM [15] framework to accelerate\n1505\tinference. For the pipeline systems comprising an ASR model integrated with LLMs, the widely adopted Whisper-Large-V3-Turbo\n1506\t[31] was utilized as the unified ASR component, achieving a Word\n1507\tError Rate (WER) of 13.69%.\n1508\t5.2.1 Quantitative Analysis. Speech-based function calling is\n1509\tsignificantly more challenging than text-based tasks, primarily due to ASR error propagation. As shown in Table 5, comparing Oracle text with pipelines reveals a substantial performance\n1510\tdrop. For instance, Qwen3-32B falls from 64.02% accuracy to 48.96%\n1511\twhen transitioning from text to speech. ASR misrecognitions of\n1512\tproper nouns and numerical values serve as the primary bottleneck,\n1513\tseverely undermining downstream intent understanding.\n1514\t\n1515\tTask complexity serves as the primary determinant of\n1516\tmodel performance. All evaluated architectures exhibit significant performance degradation as the difficulty level increases.\n1517\tSpecifically, even GPT-4o-Audio and Gemini-2.5-Pro experience\n1518\ta sharp decline in overall accuracy, dropping from 80.27% at Level 1\n1519\tto 30.31% at Level 3-2, which underscores the limitations of current\n1520\tmodels in handling NAN parameter identification and multi-turn\n1521\tsemantic completion.\n1522\tOpen-source LALMs show impressive progress but still lag\n1523\tbehind closed-source models in high-level scenarios. While\n1524\tQwen3-Omni is competitive with Gemini-2.5-Pro and GPT-4oAudio in Level 1 and 2 tasks, a significant performance gap emerges\n1525\tin Level 3. Closed-source models demonstrate superior robustness\n1526\tand generalization when handling the complex instructions and\n1527\tunseen domains characteristic of high-level tasks.\n1528\t5.2.2 Qualitative Analysis. To further investigate the failure modes\n1529\tof LALMs in SFC tasks, Table 6 presents typical error cases on the\n1530\thigh-difficulty test set. Even for GPT-4o-Audio, processing spoken\n1531\tinteractions still presents the following critical challenges: 1) Model\n1532\tHallucination. As demonstrated in case 1, when user queries\n1533\tcontain vague temporal expressions (e.g., “this Friday”), the model\n1534\ttends to hallucinate a specific time (e.g., “2025-12-12 00:00:00”) rather\n1535\tthan filling NAN as required when specific parameters are missing.\n1536\tThis over-compensation behavior can lead downstream systems to\n1537\texecute incorrect instructions. 2) Instruction Following Failure.\n1538\tAs illustrated in case 2, when faced with scenarios requiring the\n1539\tcompletion of critical missing parameters (e.g., Order ID), the model\n1540\t\n1541\t\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1542\t\n1543\tPeng et al.\n1544\t\n1545\tTable 7: Post-training results 