n1684\t74.32\n1685\t66.80\n1686\t71.44\n1687\t75.03\n1688\t\n1689\t8.49\n1690\t8.35\n1691\t8.32\n1692\t8.35\n1693\t8.30\n1694\t8.29\n1695\t\n1696\t62.50\n1697\t62.30\n1698\t62.44\n1699\t62.20\n1700\t62.38\n1701\t62.50\n1702\t\n1703\t55.58\n1704\t63.85\n1705\t63.97\n1706\t60.50\n1707\t62.14\n1708\t64.43\n1709\t\n1710\t0\n1711\t\n1712\tEM\n1713\tFine-grained\n1714\t50 100 150 200\n1715\tTraining Step\n1716\t\n1717\tSFC ID\n1718\tSFC OOD\n1719\tAPI-Bank\n1720\t\n1721\t80\n1722\t\n1723\t8.5\n1724\t\n1725\tMMSU\n1726\tCV15-EN\n1727\t\n1728\t8.4\n1729\t\n1730\t70\n1731\t60\n1732\t\n1733\t8.3\n1734\t8\n1735\t\n1736\t16\n1737\t\n1738\t32\n1739\tNumber of Rollouts (N)\n1740\t\n1741\t64\n1742\t\n1743\tASR WER (%)\n1744\t\n1745\t0.8\n1746\t0.7\n1747\t0.6\n1748\t0.5\n1749\t0.4\n1750\t\n1751\t80.38\n1752\t81.42\n1753\t81.37\n1754\t77.12\n1755\t80.31\n1756\t81.37\n1757\t\n1758\tAccuracy (%)\n1759\t\n1760\tAverage Reward\n1761\t\n1762\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B\n1763\t63.86\n1764\tGPT-4o-Audio\n1765\t80.27\n1766\tGemini-2.5-Pro\n1767\t78.05\n1768\tSpokenFC-7B (Proposed) 81.60\n1769\t\n1770\t8.2\n1771\t\n1772\tFigure 5: Ablation study Figure 6: Ablation study among\n1773\ton reward types.\n1774\tdifferent rollouts.\n1775\toccasionally reverts to a traditional chatbot mode. It provides a\n1776\tnatural language inquiry instead of outputting a structured function\n1777\tcalling sequence as required.\n1778\t\n1779\t5.3\n1780\t\n1781\tPost-Training and Ablation Study\n1782\t\n1783\tWe employ Llama-Factory [45] for SFT and MS-SWIFT [44] for\n1784\tRL. All experiments are based on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B, fine-tuning\n1785\tonly the LLM backbone via LoRA while freezing the vision encoder,\n1786\taudio encoder, and talker modules. Models are trained for one epoch\n1787\ton Nvidia 8×A800 GPUs. To evaluate generalization, we test on ASR\n1788\twith CommonVoice15-en [2], audio reasoning on MMSU [36], and\n1789\ttext function calling on API-Bank [18], level 1 subset. Experimental\n1790\tresults are in Table 7.\n1791\tSpokenFC-7B model, fine-tuned via synthetic training\n1792\tdata, demonstrates substantial performance improvements\n1793\tin both Test-ID and Test-OOD SFC tasks, as well as text-based\n1794\tfunction calling. Notably, it surpasses GPT-4o-Audio in SFC performance, and also achieves marginal gains in ASR and audio reasoning tasks compared to the base model, further validating the\n1795\tcross-modal generalization capability of the post-training.\n1796\tRL exhibits enhanced generalization and transferability\n1797\tover SFT. Although SFT facilitates notable improvements in SFC\n1798\ttasks through memorization, it fails to extend effectively to analogous text-based function call tasks. In contrast, RL encourages\n1799\tthe model to internalize the fundamental logic of semantic parsing\n1800\tand tool utilization, leading to performance enhancements in both\n1801\tspeech and text domains. Moreover, whereas SFT leads to a slight\n1802\t\n1803\tregression in ASR and Audio Reasoning capabilities, RL training\n1804\tsuccessfully addresses the issue of catastrophic forgetting, actually\n1805\tbolstering performance in these foundational tasks.\n1806\tFine-grained rewards address the sparse reward challenge\n1807\tin early-stage RL by providing dense signals, as shown in\n1808\tFigure 5. This granular feedback is particularly advantageous for\n1809\tcomplex tasks with long reasoning chains (e.g., Level 3-2); by reducing variance in policy optimization, it ensures a smoother and\n1810\tmore robust training process while improving the model’s final\n1811\tperformance.\n1812\tFP16 outperforms BF16 by leveraging its higher mantissa\n1813\tprecision to mitigate the training-inference mismatch [26].\n1814\tThis increased precision reduces cumulative rounding errors during\n1815\tlong JSON sequence generation and stabilizes training gradients\n1816\tto prevent model collapse. Consequently, FP16 achieves a superior\n1817\tbalance between task-specific optimization and the maintenance of\n1818\tgeneral capabilities.\n1819\tIncreasing the number of rollouts (𝑁 ) significantly boosts\n1820\texploration efficiency and provides more accurate baseline\n1821\testimates for advantage functions, yet it follows the law of\n1822\tdiminishing returns. While a larger model can further maximize\n1823\tin-domain performance, excessive sampling risks overfitting to specific training patterns, thereby compromising OOD generalization.\n1824\t\n1825\t6\n1826\t\n1827\tConclusion\n1828\t\n1829\tThis work introduces Spoken Function Calling as a novel perspective for task-oriented spoken semantics extraction, to evolve beyond\n1830\ttraditional closed-set SLU. Leveraging structured rule definitions,\n1831\tSFC overcomes the limitations of traditional SLU in handling opendomain tasks with ICL. Through the dataset construction and empirical evaluations, this study demonstrates that SFC significantly\n1832\timproves semantic understanding accuracy compared to traditional\n1833\tSLU for LLMs and LALMs, and SFC performance can be further\n1834\tenhanced through RL post-training with fine-grained rewards. Future work will be directed toward dataset scaling and post-training\n1835\ttechniques to further refine the model’s capabilities.\n1836\t\n1837\t\fSpoken Function Calling\n1838\t\n1839\tReferences\n1840\t[1] Sandhini Agarwal, Lama Ahmad, Jason Ai, Sam Altman, Andy Applebaum, Edwin\n1841\tArbus, Rahul K Arora, Yu Bai, Bowen Baker, Haiming Bao, et al. 2025. gpt-oss-120b\n1842\t& gpt-oss-20b model card. arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.10925 (2025).\n1843\t[2] Rosana Ardila, Megan Branson, Kelly Davis, Michael Kohler, Josh Meyer, Michael\n1844\tHenretty, Reuben Morais, Lindsay Saunders, Francis Tyers, and Gregor Weber.\n1845\t2020. Common Voice: A massively-multilingual speech corpus. In Proc. LREC.\n1846\t4218–4222.\n1847\t[3] Emanuele Bastianelli, Andrea Vanzo, Pawel Swietojanski, and Verena Rieser. 2020.\n1848\tSLURP: A spoken language understanding resource package. 