nchao Wang, Wei Deng, and\n1982\tJingchen Shu. 2025. IndexTTS2: A Breakthrough in Emotionally Expressive and\n1983\tDuration-Controlled Auto-Regressive Zero-Shot Text-to-Speech. arXiv preprint\n1984\tarXiv:2506.21619 (2025).\n1985\t[47] Yuchen Zhuang, Yue Yu, Kuan Wang, Haotian Sun, and Chao Zhang. 2023.\n1986\tToolQA: A dataset for llm question answering with external tools. In Proc.\n1987\tNeurIPS 36 (2023), 50117–50143.\n1988\t\n1989\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23f98f6f-4c63-4314-8282-ac28397ef33d","uuid":"203df517-d1ce-4318-b140-897772bb3b95","timestamp":"2026-08-07T11:04:25.318Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/2608.05126v1.txt","content":"Mult\n\nSingle-Turn Spoken Function Calling\nEnv: {\"location\": \"Paris\", \"datetime\": \"2025-04-04 02:35:28, Friday\"}\n\nEnv: {\"location\":\n\nAvailable Spoken Functions: update_reminder_time,\nﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_time, ﬁnd_location_reminder_by_location,\nupdate_recurring_reminder_content, update_location_reminder_time\n\nSpoken Function Calling: A New Perspective on Spoken\nLanguage Understanding for Large Audio Language Models\n\nAvailable Spok\nget_distance_betw\nget_navigation_dir\nﬁnd_recurring_rem\n\nUserʼs Speech Query: I need to update my weekly reminder from “Take\nout the trash” to “Replace the kitchen sponge”, starting this Friday at 7 AM.\nSFC Result: update_recurring_reminder_content(content=\"Take out the\ntrash\", new_content=”Replace the kitchen sponge\", time=\"2025-04-04\n07:00:00\", frequency=\"weekly\")\n\nYuezhang Peng∗\n\nYuxin Liu∗\n\nShanghai Jiao Tong University,\nToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\nHangzhou, China\n\nShanghai Jiao Tong University\nShanghai, China\n\nZhifu Gao\nToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\nHangzhou, China\n\nChangfeng Gao\n\nEnvironment: {\"location\":\n”Paris\", \"datetime\": \"202407-19 19:04:19, Friday\"}\n\narXiv:2608.05126v1 [cs.CL] 5 Aug 2026\n\nSpoken Language Understanding (SLU) is the core component of\ntask-oriented dialogue systems and a pivotal link in achieving seamless human-agent interaction. While traditional SLU can effectively\nextract user semantics for closed-set tasks after in-domain supervised fine-tuning, it faces significant challenges in leveraging incontext learning for open-domain tasks due to its ambiguous rule\ndefinitions. This work proposes Spoken Function Calling (SFC), a\nnovel semantic understanding perspective that optimizes semantic\nunderstanding with structured rule definitions, to evolve beyond\ntraditional closed-set SLU. Specifically, we curate and extend a suite\nof spoken functions based on traditional SLU datasets, construct a\nmulti-agent system to synthesize the SFC-Bench dataset, evaluate\nthe performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Audio Language Models (LALMs), and enhance the SFC capabilities of\nLALMs through post-training. Experiments demonstrate that SFC\noutperforms traditional SLU, substantially enhancing the semantic\nextraction accuracy for LLMs and LALMs. 1\n\nCCS Concepts\n• Computing methodologies → Natural language processing;\nSpeech recognition; Discourse, dialogue and pragmatics.\n\nKeywords\nSpoken Language Understanding, Spoken Function Calling, Large\nAudio Language Models, In-Context Learning\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nSpoken Language Understanding (SLU) parses the semantic information of user queries through intent classification and slot\nfilling to facilitate the execution of downstream tasks [35]. This\nparadigm has been extensively deployed in voice assistants across\nsmartphones, smart homes, and automotive systems [3, 21, 25].\nHowever, traditional SLU tasks operate under the assumption that\nmodels possess prior knowledge of all predefined intent and slot\nschemas. While models can memorize these rules via in-domain\nSupervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) to achieve superior performance,\n∗ Equal Contribution.\n† Corresponding author.\n1 The code and dataset are available at https://github.com/QwenAudio/FunResearch/\n\ntree/main/SpokenFC.\n\n{Intent: add_schedule, Slot:\ncontent=dentist appointment,\ntime or start_time ? = … ,\nlocation=Paris}\n\nXie Chen†\n\nI maybe add a schedule\nToken Foundry, AlibabaCould\nfor Group\na denAst appointment on\nJuly 20th at 3 PM in Paris?\nHangzhou, China\n\nAbstract\n\nAvailable Intents: add_schedule,\nadd_recurring_schedule, …\nAvailable Slots: content, Ame,\nlocaAon, start_Ame, frequency, …\n\nXiangang Li User’s Speech Query:\n\nUserʼs Speech\n123 Queen’s Road\n\nToken Foundry, Alibaba Group\nBeijing, China\nSLU Context\nSLU Confusion\n\nSFC Context\n\nLALM\n\n❓\n\nSFC Success\n\nShanghai Jiao Tong University,\nadd_schedule (\n✅\nShanghai Innovation\nInstitute\ncontent=denAst\nappointment,\nAme=2024-07-20 15:00:00,\nShanghai, China\nlocaAon=Paris)\n\nAvailable Functions:\nadd_schedule (content, time,\nlocation),\nadd_recurring_conference\n(content, start_time, frequency),…\n\nSpeech Query: Could I maybe add a schedule for a dentist appointment on July 20th at 3 PM in Paris?\nEnvironmental Context: Date=2024-07-19 19:04:19, Loc=Paris\n\nTraditional SLU Task\nAvailable Intent & Slot Pool\nadd_schedule\nother intents…\n\nlocation\n\ntime\n\nadd_recur_schedule\n\n\"July 20th\nat 3 PM\"\n\n？\n\n？\n\nother slots…\n\nProposed SFC Task\nAvailable Function Pool\n• add_schedule(content, time, location)\n• add_recur_schedule(content,start_time,\nfrequency) ...\n\nSelected Function:\ncontent\n\nstart_time\n\nSLU CONFUSION\n\nAmbiguous rule definition\n\nSlot values face ambiguity\nwhen mapping to overlapping\ncandidates (e.g., \"time\" vs.\n\"start_time\")\n\n• add_schedule (content, time, location)\ndentist\nappoint.\n\n2024-0720 15:00:00\n\nParis\n\nSFC SUCCESS\n\nPrecise structured binding\n\nParameter values are\ndetermined by the specific\nschema constraints within the\nselected function\n\nFigure 1: Comparison of traditional SLU and proposed SFC.\nSFC resolves parameter ambiguity through a precise and\nstructured function definition.\n\nthey struggle with open vocabulary and out-of-domain tasks [25].\nEven state-of-the-art (SOTA) Large Language Models (LLMs) and\nLarge Audio-Language Models (LALMs) fail to achieve satisfactory\nresults on such tasks through In-Context Learning (ICL) [4]. In\nthis context, we define “open-domain\" as the capability of a model\nto understand and execute tasks through ICL based on dynamic\nfunctional descriptions, rather than relying on in-domain SFT to\npre-memorize SLU schemas.\nRecently, function calling has emerged as a transformative paradigm for semantic parsing and task execution [23, 24]. Unlike\ntraditional SLU, which requires agents to pre-memorize rigid rules,\nfunction calling enables LLMs to interpret function definitions\ndynamically through ICL and structured prompts. This approach facilitates the autonomous invocation of the Model Context Protocol\n(MCP) [11] for diverse open-domain tasks, bypassing the need for\nSFT on specific functions. Inspired by the success of function calling\nin open-domain scenarios, we investigate a pivotal question: Can\nSpoken Function Calling (SFC) evolve beyond traditional SLU\nto enhance the semantic extraction performance of agents?\n\n\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nSingle-Turn Spoken Function Calling\n\nPeng et al.\n\nMulti-Turn Spoken Function Calling\n\nEnv: {\"location\": \"Paris\", \"datetime\": \"2025-04-04 02:35:28, Friday\"}\nAvailable Spoken Functions: update_reminder_time,\nﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_time, ﬁnd_location_reminder_by_location,\nupdate_recurring_reminder_content, update_location_reminder_time\nUserʼs Speech Query: I need to update my weekly reminder from “Take\nout the trash” to “Replace the kitchen sponge”, starting this Friday at 7 AM.\nSFC Result: update_recurring_reminder_content(content=\"Take out the\ntrash\", new_content=”Replace the kitchen sponge\", time=\"2025-04-04\n07:00:00\", frequency=\"weekly\")\n\nSingle-Turn Multiple Spoken Function Calling\nEnv: {\"location\": \"Seoul\", \"datetime\": \"2023-02-13 19:04:19, Monday\"}\n\nEnv: {\"location\": \"Hong Kong\", \"datetime\": \"2026-05-31 03:00:33, Sunday\"}\nAvailable Spoken Functions:\nget_distance_between_locations, update_recurring_reminder_time,\nget_navigation_directions, delete_recurring_reminder,\nfind_recurring_reminder_by_keyword, get_travel_time_between_locations\n\nStage 1\nUserʼs Speech Query: Find my reminder, tell me the travel time from\n123 Queen’s Road Central, Hong Kong to Hong Kong International Airport.\nSFC Result:\nget_travel_time_between_locations(location1=“123 Queen’s Road Central,\nHong Kong”, location2=“Hong Kong International Airport”),\nﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_keyword(keyword=\"NAN\")\n\nAvailable Spoken Functions:\nskip_reminder, search_wikipedia, ﬁnd_reminder_by_keyword,\nupdate_location_reminder_time, update_recurring_reminder_content\nUserʼs Speech Query: Could you search Wikipedia for the history of the\nEiffel Tower and then change my weekly reminder from “Check the news” to\n“Read the latest article about it,” starting tomorrow morning at 8 AM?\nSFC Result: search_wikipedia(query=\"history of the Eiﬀel Tower\"),\nupdate_recurring_reminder_content(content=\"Check the news\",\nnew_content=\"Read the latest article about the Eiﬀel Tower\", time=\"2023-0214 08:00:00\", frequency=\"weekly\")\n\nStage 2\nUserʼs Speech Query: Reminder about gym.\nSFC Result:\nget_travel_time_between_locations(location1=“123 Queen’s Road Central,\nHong Kong”, location2=“Hong Kong International Airport”),\nﬁnd_recurring_reminder_by_keyword(keyword=\"gym\")\n\nFigure 2: Example of Multi-Intent and Multi-Turn Spoken Function Calling tasks.\nHowever, addressing this question presents a critical challenge:\nthe lack of SFC datasets that correspond to traditional SLU\ntasks. Specifically, existing LLMs function call datasets focus on\ncomplex reasoning within code or mathematics, which does not\nalign with the requirements of traditional SLU. Furthermore, queries\nand tools designed for text-centric tasks lack the inherent uncertainty and linguistic flexibility of human speech.\nIn this work, we leverage SLU datasets and a multi-agent system\nto construct SFC-Bench, the first large-scale SFC dataset. Through\ncomparative experiments, we demonstrate that SFC is a more suitable perspective for LALM agents than traditional SLU, as shown\nin Figure 1. We further establish a benchmark to investigate the\nperformance and limitations of LLMs and LALMs on SFC tasks of\nvarying levels. In summary, our key contributions are listed below.\n• Perspective Shift: We propose Spoken Function Calling as a\nnovel perspective for task-oriented spoken semantics extraction,\nto extend traditional closed-set SLU based on intent classification\nand slot filling in the agent era.\n• Post-training Optimization: We investigate reinforcement\nlearning (RL) based post-training strategies for LALMs and propose a fine-grained reward method. Leveraging these strategies,\nwe develop SpokenFC-7B through post-training on a comprehensive synthetic dataset.\n• Dataset Construction: We introduce SFC-Bench, the first largescale SFC dataset. Starting from a curated tool set of 300 spoken\nfunctions derived from existing SLU datasets, we employ a multiagent system to synthesize multi-level queries and labels.\n• Empirical Validation: Our experiments confirm that: 1) SFC\nserves as a more effective semantic understanding method for\n\nLALMs compared to traditional SLU. 2) Even SOTA LALMs still\nface challenges in high-level SFC tasks involving multi-intent and\nambiguous semantics. 3) SpokenFC-7B outperforms powerful\nclosed-source models on SFC tasks while maintaining robust\nperformance on normal tasks such as speech recognition and\naudio reasoning.\n\n2 Related Work\n2.1 Traditional SLU\n2.1.1 LLMs for SLU. LLMs pre-trained under the scaling law [14]\nexhibit emergent reasoning and generalization capabilities across\nvarious Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, successfully addressing complex problems in question-answering [47], mathematics [20], and coding [6]. Recent research explores the application\nof LLMs to SLU tasks. Early research [9] investigates using ICL\nfor intent classification and slot filling, demonstrating that while\nLLMs perform commendably in intent classification, they still face\nchallenges with the more complex structural definitions of slot filling. Whisma [17] employs a Whisper [31] encoder and Llama-3 [8]\ndecoder with modality alignment training, enabling zero-shot SLU\ncapabilities that outperform traditional pipeline methods. Furthermore, MAC-SLU [25] establishes a unified benchmark for LLMs and\nLALMs on SLU, indicating that while current LLMs and LALMs can\nperform well on sub-tasks via ICL (e.g., isolated intent classification\nor slot filling), their overall accuracy remains low and significantly\nlags behind models optimized through SFT.\n2.1.2 SLU Datasets. SLU datasets primarily include single-intent\nbenchmarks such as ATIS, SNIPS, FSC, and SLURP [3, 7, 10, 21],\n\n\fSpoken Function Calling\n\nas well as multi-intent datasets like MixATIS and MixSNIPS [29].\nThe ATIS [10] dataset focuses on simple voice queries for flight\ninformation with only 16 intent categories. SNIPS [7] and FSC [21]\ntarget smart home scenarios but remain limited in scope, featuring\nonly 7 and 13 intents, respectively. The simplicity and homogeneity\nof ATIS, SNIPS, and FSC allow even non-LLM-based SLU models\nto achieve over 95% overall accuracy [28]. SLURP [3] is currently\nthe most widely used large-scale SLU dataset, expanding the intent\ncategories to 46. In the context of Chinese SLU, MAC-SLU [25] is\nderived from text commands in the automotive cabin with speech\nsynthesized via Text-to-Speech (TTS) models. MAC-SLU comprises\n8 domains, 81 intents, and 192 slots, including multi-intent data\nwith up to five concurrent intents, surpassing previous datasets\nin both diversity and complexity. Furthermore, although dialogue\nstate tracking [38, 39] is inherently linked with SLU, and existing benchmarks like MTOP [16] and MultiWOZ [5] have adopted\nfunction-calling structures, their scope is restricted to text-based,\nmulti-turn dialogues. In contrast, this work concentrates on endto-end semantic understanding specifically for the speech modality\nand the precise invocation of spoken tools, presenting fundamental\ndifferences in both input modality and task objectives.\n\n2.2\n\nFunction Calling\n\n2.2.1 LLMs for Function Calling. Function calling empowers LLMs\nwith tool-use capabilities to overcome inherent limitations such\nas hallucinations [13] and the lack of real-time information. ReAct [43] represents a milestone in function calling, proposing an\ninterleaved reasoning and acting paradigm. This approach guides\nmodels to generate reasoning traces before executing an action,\nleveraging Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [37] to plan tool calls and enhancing the model’s ability to solve complex problems. Toolformer\n[32] introduces a self-supervised learning method that automatically inserts API calls into text and filters low-quality samples,\nteaching the model how to invoke external APIs without extensive\nhuman annotation. ToolRL [27] utilizes RL training to bolster the\nreasoning capabilities of LLMs during function calling, achieving\nperformance superior to both base and SFT models.\n2.2.2 Function Calling Datasets. Current function calling datasets\ninclude the Berkeley Function Calling Leaderboard (BFCL) [24],\nAPI-Bank [18], ToolBench [30], and ToolACE [19]. BFCL [24] evaluates function calling using a comprehensive dataset encompassing\nHuggingFace, TorchHub, and TensorHub APIs. API-Bank [18] provides the first benchmark specifically for tool-augmented LLMs,\ncovering capabilities ranging from single-turn queries to multi-turn\ndialogue management. ToolLLM [30] constructs ToolBench, a largescale instruction-tuning dataset featuring over 16,000 functional\nRESTful APIs. ToolACE [19] utilizes an automated agentic pipeline\nto generate accurate, complex, and diverse tool-learning data, enhancing function calling through data augmentation. Despite the\nproliferation of these datasets, they remain focused on code, mathematics, and complex reasoning, which do not align with spoken\nsemantic understanding scenarios. Furthermore, speech-centric\ndatasets like StepEval-Audio-Toolcall [40] support only four categories of tools, which is insufficient for a comprehensive evaluation\nof LALMs’ performance in SFC tasks.\n\nMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\n3 Methodology\n3.1 Spoken Function Calling\nWithin the paradigm of ICL for LLMs and LALMs, the SLU based\non intent classification and slot filling can be conceptualized as a\nsemantic extraction and generation process grounded in instruction\nfollowing. Its task mapping relationship can be defined as:\n𝐹 SLU : (𝐴, 𝑆 int, 𝑆 slot ) → 𝑌SLU,\n\n(1)\n\nwhere 𝐴 denotes the input raw speech signal, 𝑆 int and 𝑆 slot denote\nthe predefined sets of candidate intent and slot labels, respectively.\nThe output 𝑌SLU is represented as a set of semantic frames:\n𝑌SLU = {𝐼 1, . . . , 𝐼𝑛 , (𝑠 1, 𝑣 1 ), . . . , (𝑠𝑚 , 𝑣𝑚 )}.\n\n(2)\n\nIn this structure, 𝐼𝑖 ∈ 𝑆 int is the 𝑖-th identified intent label, while\n𝑠 𝑗 ∈ 𝑆 slot and 𝑣 𝑗 represent the corresponding slot name and its\nassociated value, respectively.\nTo accommodate the requirements for agents to handle opendomain SLU with customized intents and slots and to circumvent\nthe reliance on memorizing rigid rules through SFT, this work\nproposes a novel perspective: Spoken Function Calling. SFC aims\nto map spoken instructions into a sequence of directly executable\nstructured API calls:\n𝐹 SFC : (𝐴, 𝐸, 𝐷 func ) → 𝑌SFC .\n\n(3)\n\nIn this definition, 𝐸 explicitly introduces the environmental context,\nwhich serves to compensate for semantic omissions common in\nspoken language; 𝐷 func represents the function definition schema\nfollowing the OpenFunction protocol. The detailed output 𝑌SFC is\nmanifested as a set of structured function calls:\n𝑌SFC = {𝑓𝑖 (𝑘𝑖,1 = 𝑣𝑖,1, 𝑘𝑖,2 = 𝑣𝑖,2, . . . , 𝑘𝑖,𝑚𝑖 = 𝑣𝑖,𝑚𝑖 )}𝑛𝑖=1,\n\n(4)\n\nwhere 𝑓𝑖 is the retrieved function name, and 𝑘𝑖,𝑗 and 𝑣𝑖,𝑗 represent\nparameter keys and values. If a parameter is missing, it is assigned\na value of NAN to trigger subsequent completion mechanisms. Compared to traditional SLU, the SFC perspective offers the following\nsignificant advantages: 1) Robustness of Structured Constraints:\nThe schema constraints introduced via 𝐷 func serve as strong prior\nknowledge, effectively resolving the boundary ambiguity issues encountered by traditional SLU when performing complex parameter\nextraction. 2) Coupled Intent and Slot Parsing: The function\ndefinition 𝐷 func simultaneously constrains the parsing of both function names (intents) and parameter values (slots). This addresses a\ncritical limitation in traditional SLU, where the correlation between\nintents and slots is difficult to define explicitly.\n\n3.2\n\nRL-based Post-training\n\nThis study adopts Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) [34]\nas the core algorithm for post-training. Compared to the traditional\nProximal Policy Optimization (PPO) [33] algorithm, a significant\nadvantage of GRPO is that it eliminates the need to train an additional large-scale value function model and substantially reduces\nthe memory footprint and computational overhead.\nFor each spoken query 𝑞, GRPO samples a group of outputs\n{𝑜 1, 𝑜 2, . . . , 𝑜𝐺 } from the old policy 𝜋𝜃 old . The original objective\n\n\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nPen