ns cover a broad spectrum of operations,\nextraction. By providing positive feedback when a sub-task is perranging from standard CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) actions\nformed correctly, we incentivize the model to master individual\nto domain-specific complex logic. Thirdly, we conducted manual\ncomponents before achieving full function call synthesis. The final\nRefinement, where every generated function underwent rigorous\nreward 𝑅Fine-Grained is normalized to the [−1, 1] range by scaling the\nhuman filtering and correction.\n\n\fSpoken Function Calling\n\nMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nl\n\nGenefora\nIn\n(22)\n\nRemin\n(24) der\n\nCo\nnf\n(20eren\n) ce\n\nM\n(11ovie\n)\n\nket\nTic(12)\n\nShoOnline\np\n(18)ping\n\nPersonal\nAssistant\n(90)\n\nAlarm\n(14)\n\nLife\nServices\n(45)\n\nFood\nDelivery\n(14)\n\nr\nnda\nCale(20)\n\nInformation\nRetrieval\n(45)\n\nTodo\n(12)\n\nan\nRestaur\n(13)\n\nCommunication\n(56)\n\nMe\ns\n(9) sage\n\ni\nTax11)\n(\nT\n(13rain\n)\n\nPho\n(12)ne\n\nhip\nt S(10)\n\nTravel &\nTransport\n(64)\n\nPla\n(14)ne\n\nHotel\n(16)\n\nApp\nCom4m) .\n(1\n\neo\nVidCall )\n(10\nl\ni\nEm1a1)\n(\n\nMetrics\n\nTrain\n\nTest-ID\n\nTest-OOD\n\nLevel 1\nAvg. Question Length\nAvg. Answer Length\nAvg. Audio Length\n\n774.58\n20.68\n7.30\n\n728.41\n18.35\n6.62\n\n704.69\n13.34\n6.69\n\nLevel 2\nAvg. Question Length\nAvg. Answer Length\nAvg. Audio Length\n\n708.80\n30.14\n10.65\n\n707.85\n28.51\n10.28\n\n744.68\n26.93\n10.21\n\nLevel 3-1\nAvg. Question Length\nAvg. Answer Length\nAvg. Audio Length\n\n792.51\n40.92\n8.70\n\n808.55\n44.93\n8.65\n\n732.21\n27.68\n7.66\n\nLevel 3-2\nAvg. Question Length\nAvg. Answer Length\nAvg. Audio Length\n\n827.57\n73.96\n13.15\n\n806.98\n63.17\n11.55\n\n793.87\n52.71\n11.32\n\nFigure 4: The domain distribution (left) and detailed statistics (right) of average question lengths (tokens), answer lengths\n(tokens), and audio lengths (seconds) across different levels of splits.\nTable 1: Sample distribution in the SFC-Bench dataset.\n\nLevel Train\n\nTest-ID\n\nTest-OOD\n\nMulti- MultiIntent Turn\n\nL1\nL2\nL3-1\nL3-2\n\n2,647\n817\n447\n588\n\n902\n480\n317\n287\n\n321\n94\n126\n52\n\n✗\n✓\n✗\n✓\n\n✗\n✗\n✓\n✓\n\nTotal\n\n4,499\n\n1,986\n\n593\n\n–\n\n–\n\nIn total, we curated 300 daily-life oriented spoken tools spanning\n5 domains and 21 scenarios, as presented in Figure 4 (left). To\nevaluate the model’s generalization capabilities, we partitioned the\nfunction set into 232 In-Distribution (ID) and 68 Out-of-Distribution\n(OOD) functions. Although the diversity and inherent complexity\nof the Test-OOD set are slightly lower than those of the Test-ID\nset due to this distribution, it does not compromise the validity of\nthe experiments in assessing the model’s training generalization.\nAll subsequent training was conducted solely on data synthesized\nfrom the ID subset, while evaluations were performed on both ID\nand OOD test sets to verify robustness and transferability.\n\n4.2\n\nSFC-Bench Construction\n\nWorkflow Overview. The workflow for SFC-Bench construction\nis depicted in Figure 3. We synthesize the dataset by leveraging\nfunction metadata curated from SLU benchmarks, integrated with\ndiverse environmental contexts to facilitate data augmentation.\n\nMulti-Agent Generator. Central to this workflow is the MultiAgent Generator, powered by GPT-OSS-120B [1] with custom system prompts, which comprises three primary functional agents: 1)\nQuery Agent: Generates text queries based on pre-selected functions and environmental context. For Level 3 data involving ambiguous semantics, an additional step of fuzzy rewriting is performed.\n2) Label Agent: Produces accurate function calls based on the chosen functions, environmental context, and queries. For ambiguous\nLevel 3 queries, it generates function calls containing NAN parameters to assess potential hallucination. 3) Speech Agent: Introduces\nspoken language features (e.g., redundancy, self-corrections) and\nverbalizes technical formats (e.g., converting “abc@gmail.com\" to\n“abc at gmail dot com\"). Finally, it employs IndexTTS-2 [46] to synthesize speech queries by utilizing speaker timbre profiles from the\nLibriSpeech dataset [22] to mimic diverse vocal characteristics. To\nensure experimental rigor, distinct speaker identities are assigned\nto the training, validation, and test sets, maintaining strict speaker\nisolation across the data splits.\nValidator. Data synthesized by the Multi-Agent Generator undergoes inspection and filtering by the validator agent to ensure\nlogical and grammatical soundness, as well as consistency between\nqueries and labels. For the test set, an additional human validation phase is conducted to guarantee quality. The distribution of\nthe SFC-Bench is summarized in Table 1. We generated over 7,000\nSFC data samples, covering a range of Multi-Intent and Multi-Turn\nscenarios.\nStatistical data in Figure 4 (right) demonstrate a trend in complexity as difficulty levels escalate. The average answer length\nsurges from 20 tokens at Level 1 to over 70 tokens at Level 3-2,\nhighlighting the stringent requirements for structured parsing in\nmulti-intent and multi-turn semantic completion tasks. Concurrently, the mean audio duration increases from about 7 seconds\n\n\fMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nPeng et al.\n\nTable 2: Qualitative comparison between SFC and traditional SLU tasks. The Result column indicates the success (✓) or failure\n(✗) of each task based on the ground truth. Blue text highlights specific discrepancies.\nTask\n\nModel Prediction\n\nGround Truth\n\nResult\n\nCase 1: Hey, delete my doctor appointment reminder for tomorrow morning at 8 AM, I’m in a rush!\nSFC\nSLU\n\ndelete_reminder(content = \"doctor appointment\", time = \"202503-20 08:00:00\")\n\"intent\": \"delete_reminder\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"doctor appointment\" }\n\ndelete_reminder(content = \"doctor appointment\", time = \"202503-20 08:00:00\")\n\"intent\": \"delete_reminder\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"doctor appointment\", \"time\": \"2025-03-20 08:00:00\" }\n\n✓\n✗\n\nCase 2: Um, could I maybe add a schedule for a dentist appointment on July 20th at 3 PM in Paris?\nSFC\nSLU\n\nadd_schedule(content = \"dentist appointment\", time = \"2024-0720 15:00:00\", location=\"Paris\")\n\"intent\": \"add_schedule\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"dentist appointment\",\n\"start_time\": \"2024-07-20 15:00:00\", \"location\": \"Paris\" }\n\nTable 3: Experimental results of SLU and SFC tasks. The metrics include Intent (Function Name) accuracy, Slot (Parameter\nValue) F1 Score, and Overall Accuracy across ID and OOD test\nsets. Best results are in bold.\nTest-ID\nTask Intent\n\nSlot\n\nTest-OOD\nOverall Intent\n\nSlot\n\nOverall\n\n69.73\n85.70\n\n97.20\n99.07\n\n92.52\n95.83\n\n83.80\n91.90\n\n84.26\n92.68\n\n70.44\n87.03\n\n96.88\n99.07\n\n90.24\n94.48\n\n82.24\n90.03\n\nQwen2.5-7B, Text Input\nSLU\n93.90 78.73\nICL\nSFC\n91.57 85.41\n\n61.86\n73.95\n\n93.15\n93.46\n\n82.71\n87.58\n\n70.57\n75.70\n\n68.63\n83.15\n\n93.77\n99.38\n\n87.42\n94.48\n\n77.00\n89.72\n\nQwen2.5-Omni-7B, Speech Input\nSLU\n94.46 68.06\n53.88\nICL\nSFC\n93.02 79.69 63.86\n\n95.64\n97.20\n\n69.12\n80.43\n\n50.16\n68.54\n\n96.57\n100.0\n\n74.79\n79.52\n\n57.01\n67.29\n\nQwen3-8B, Text Input\nSLU\n96.01 85.20\nICL\nSFC\n97.56 91.83\nSFT\n\nSFT\n\nSFT\n\nSLU\nSFC\n\nSLU\nSFC\n\nSLU\nSFC\n\n96.74\n97.67\n\n95.79\n96.78\n\n98.56\n99.45\n\n84.94\n90.97\n\n85.50\n87.80\n\n75.61\n80.71\n\nTable 4: Comparison of average confidence (Log Probabilities)\nbetween SFC and SLU tasks.\n\nSFC Confidence\nSLU Confidence\n\nTest-ID\n\nTest-OOD\n\n-1.62\n-1.87\n\n-1.57\n-1.72\n\nto 13 seconds. This increased audio load substantially exacerbates\nthe challenges of ASR error propagation and long-range semantic\nalignment, which constitute the primary obstacles to maintaining\nrobustness in LALMs when processing complex instructions.\n\nadd_schedule(content = \"dentist appointment\", time = \"2024-0720 15:00:00\", location=\"Paris\")\n\"intent\": \"add_schedule\", \"slots\": { \"content\": \"dentist appointment\",\n\"time\": \"2024-07-20 15:00:00\", \"location\": \"Paris\" }\n\n5\n\n✓\n✗\n\nExperiments\n\nExperiments are organized into three components: 1) SLU-SFC\nComparative Analysis: compare the performance of models when\nprocessing identical semantic datasets under traditional SLU and\nour proposed SFC perspective. 2) Benchmark Study: evaluate\nLLMs and LALMs’ performance on SFC-Bench across varying levels\nof difficulty. 3) Post-Training: enhance the SFC capabilities of\nLALMs by integrating SFT and RL techniques, while maintaining\nmodels’ general capabilities.\n\n5.1\n\nSLU vs SFC\n\nWe initially conducted comparative experiments using the Level 1\nsubset of SFC-Bench. These experiments involved both ICL and SFT\nacross LLMs Qwen2.5-7B and Qwen3-8B [42], and LALM Qwen2.5Omni-7B [41]. For LLMs, the inputs consisted of textual user queries,\nwhereas the LALM received the corresponding speech input with\nthe same context. All models were deployed by vLLM [15] framework to accelerate inference. In the SFT phase, we utilized the LlamaFactory [45] training framework and applied Low-Rank Adaptation\n(LoRA) [12] for parameter-efficient fine-tuning.\n5.1.1 Quantitative Analysis. SFC consistently outperforms the\ntraditional SLU, across both text and audio modalities. As\nshown in Table 3, SFC demonstrates an improvement of 5–18%\nin both audio and text modalities. These gains are primarily attributed to the more granular and rigorous function definitions\ninherent in SFC, which enhance the accuracy of parameter value\n(analogous to the slot filling in traditional SLU), thereby boosting\nthe overall performance. In contrast, SFC exhibits no significant\nadvantage in function name (equivalent to intent classification in\nSLU). The reason is that both function name and intent classification are relatively straightforward classification problems that do\nnot necessitate complex rule constraints.\n5.1.2 Qualitative Analysis. Core advantage of SFC over traditional SLU lies in its more structured rule definitions. SFC\nutilizes explicit schemas for tools and parameters; for any given tool,\nthe parameter rules are fixed. For instance, as shown in Table 2, the\ndelete_reminder tool is strictly defined with two parameters: content\nand time. In traditional SLU, however, the rules for intent classification and slot filling are often decoupled. For a specific intent, the\n\n\fSpoken Function Calling\n\nMM’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nTable 5: Experimental results on the SFC Test-ID. The metrics include Intent (Function Name) Accuracy, Slot (Parameter Value)\nF1 Score, and Overall Accuracy. Best results are in bold.\nLevel 1\n\nLevel 2\n\nLevel 3-1\n\nLevel 3-2\n\nAverage (Turn-Level)\n\nIntent Slot Overall\n\nIntent Slot Overall\n\nIntent Slot Overall\n\nIntent Slot Overall\n\nIntent Slot Overall\n\nLLM\n\nLlama-3.1-8B-Instruct\nQwen2.5-7B\nQwen3-8B\nQwen3-32B\n\n68.40 56.78\n91.57 85.41\n97.56 92.99\n94.01 91.24\n\n34.48\n73.95\n87.69\n81.71\n\n57.17 44.58\n87.97 87.02\n94.09 93.63\n92.41 93.92\n\n7.59\n61.81\n80.39\n81.22\n\n50.79 52.84\n85.17 77.02\n95.27 80.99\n92.43 81.44\n\n9.46\n27.44\n31.34\n37.33\n\n52.61 55.52\n79.09 80.58\n83.62 80.56\n87.11 82.30\n\n5.23\n19.86\n22.95\n26.04\n\n64.71 50.52\n89.55 78.87\n94.42 83.77\n92.97 84.42\n\n20.86\n54.89\n62.83\n64.02\n\nASR +\nLLM\n\nLlama-3.1-8B-Instruct\nQwen2.5-Omni\nQwen3-8B\nQwen3-32B\n\n68.51 45.29\n93.02 79.69\n97.67 78.77\n94.90 78.90\n\n27.61\n63.86\n68.07\n64.63\n\n56.96 37.47\n82.49 76.74\n92.83 76.58\n92.19 77.94\n\n5.91\n47.05\n49.58\n51.27\n\n49.84 45.42\n80.44 73.07\n94.64 73.95\n91.17 78.49\n\n5.99\n18.61\n17.98\n23.77\n\n44.95 49.53\n40.07 66.76\n86.41 74.40\n86.76 78.70\n\n1.74\n7.32\n13.94\n16.63\n\n63.88 42.97\n84.02 73.85\n94.29 72.97\n92.66 76.01\n\n15.90\n48.80\n47.27\n48.96\n\nLALM\n\nQwen2.5